---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../lib/site";
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="What Orpheon is, and how the journal is written.">
  <article class="max-w-2xl">
    <p class="kicker">Method</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 font-serif text-5xl tracking-tight">About Orpheon</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 font-serif text-2xl italic text-ink-soft">
      A deterministic change-detection pipeline. AI is used only for semantic understanding and documentation — never as a nightly rewrite of a README.
    </p>
    <div class="prose prose-journal mt-10">
      <p>
        {site.title} is a continuously maintained technical record of work by {site.ownerName}.
        It is not a conventional portfolio. Someone discovering a project should be able to choose their depth:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Two minutes</strong> — Explain Like I'm 15</li>
        <li><strong>I'm an engineer</strong> — Technical Deep Dive</li>
        <li><strong>I want the theory</strong> — References & Ideas</li>
        <li><strong>I want the making of</strong> — Build History</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>How the night run works</h2>
      <p>
        During the day, commits accumulate and Orpheon stays quiet. Around 23:30 Asia/Kolkata a GitHub Action compares each tracked repository's HEAD to the last processed SHA. Identical SHAs are skipped entirely — no diff, no model call, no regeneration.
      </p>
      <p>
        When HEAD has moved, Orpheon reads the accumulated diff, a little surrounding context, and any human <code>.orpheon.yml</code>. It classifies the day's work, updates only the pages that need it, appends a build-history entry, and commits:
      </p>
      <pre><code>orpheon: nightly journal update YYYY-MM-DD</code></pre>
      <p>
        Git is the source of truth. Human metadata is authoritative. The model provides interpretation. Orpheon provides continuity.
      </p>
      <h2>What it will not do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Run after every push</li>
        <li>Invent papers, APIs, metrics, or users</li>
        <li>Publish secrets or private URLs</li>
        <li>Rewrite old build-history entries</li>
        <li>Treat an unchanged repository as new material</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
